> API and interpretation

Malformed generators reject before indexing; malformed candidates return false.
Membership validates public BSGS structure, but cannot certify arbitrary
manually edited maps as an authentic closed group. Order overflow and actual
work/history policies throw. The chain is not a materialized list of every group
member.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Sims, Computational methods in the study of permutation groups (1970)|https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780080129754500205]

